## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to model simple domain data, perform file-based persistence with Codable, and drive UI with SwiftUI while handling dates and basic filtering.

## Key Concepts
- SwiftUI List and navigation
- Codable JSON persistence to Documents
- File I/O and error handling
- Date arithmetic for overdue
- Lightweight test scaffolding

## Code Example
```swift
struct Task: Codable {
  let id: UUID
  var title: String
  var dueDate: Date
  var isDone: Bool
}

final class TaskStore {
  private var tasks: [Task] = []
  private let url: URL
  init(filename: String = "tasks.json") {
    let dir = FileManager.default.urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask).first!
    self.url = dir.appendingPathComponent(filename)
    load()
  }
  func load() {
    if let data = try? Data(contentsOf: url), let decoded = try? JSONDecoder().decode([Task].self, from: data) {
      tasks = decoded
    }
  }
  func save() {
    if let data = try? JSONEncoder().encode(tasks) {
      try? data.write(to: url)
    }
  }
  // add/edit/delete helpers...
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you migrate to Core Data if the feature set grows? 
- How would you detect and recover from partial write failures?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical iOS fundamentals: Codable data models, file-based persistence with atomic writes, and SwiftUI data flow. It also exercises UX concerns like debounce for responsiveness and accessibility considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Codable data model for Note with title, body, tags, and date
- File I/O in Documents with atomic write pattern
- SwiftUI state management using @Published and binding for filters
- Combine debouncing for search input
- Tag-based filtering logic and accessibility labeling

## Code Example
```javascript
import Foundation

struct Note: Codable, Identifiable {
  var id: UUID = UUID()
  var title: String
  var body: String
  var tags: [String]
  var date: Date = Date()
}

final class NotesStore: ObservableObject {
  @Published var notes: [Note] = []
  private let fileURL: URL = FileManager.default
    .urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask)[0]
    .appendingPathComponent("notes.json")

  init() { load() }

  func load() {
    guard let data = try? Data(contentsOf: fileURL) else { return }
    if let loaded = try? JSONDecoder().decode([Note].self, from: data) {
      notes = loaded
    }
  }

  func save() {
    let tempURL = fileURL.appendingPathExtension("tmp")
    guard let data = try? JSONEncoder().encode(notes) else { return }
    try? data.write(to: tempURL)
    try? FileManager.default.replaceItemAtURL(fileURL, withItemAtURL: tempURL)
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to thousands of notes while keeping memory usage low?
- How would you test atomic write correctness and debounce behavior?
- How would you migrate to Core Data or add cloud sync later?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses offline-first design, delta-based syncing, and conflict handling under intermittent connectivity, which are critical in collaboration apps used by teams.

## Key Concepts

- Core Data data model for Comment and Patch objects
- Delta/patch format including baseVersion, changes, and timestamps
- Reconciliation strategy (latest-writer-wins or server-validated merges)
- Robust test plan for offline edits, re-connect, and concurrency

## Code Example

```javascript
// Swift-like pseudocode: apply a patch to a Comment
func apply(patch: Patch, to comment: inout Comment) {
    // Validate base version matches current state
    guard patch.baseVersion == comment.version else {
        // Handle conflict resolution
        resolveConflict(patch: patch, comment: &comment)
        return
    }

    // Apply delta changes
    for change in patch.changes {
        switch change.type {
        case .insert:
            comment.text.insert(change.text, at: change.position)
        case .delete:
            comment.text.removeSubrange(change.range)
        case .replace:
            comment.text.replaceSubrange(change.range, with: change.text)
        }
    }

    // Update version and timestamps
    comment.version += 1
    comment.updatedAt = Date()
}
```

